<p>The optimal choice is whichever of these two options yields the largest =
amount of money.</p>
<p>Notice this discussion highlights the existence of subproblems, and an <=
strong>optimal substructure</strong> where we need to know the total amount=
 stolen up to the two previous houses, before determining the total amount =
we can steal up to the current house.</p>
<p>This means we should try using <strong>DP</strong> to solve this problem=
. Let=E2=80=99s say <code>dp[i]</code> represents the maximum amount we=E2=
=80=99re able to steal by the time we reach house <code>i</code>. Based on =
our previous discussion, we know that:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>dp[i]</code> =3D max(profit if we skip house <code>i</code>, profi=
t if we rob house <code>i</code>) =3D <code>max(dp[i - 1], houses[i] + dp[i=
 - 2])</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Once the DP array is populated using the above formula, we can just retu=
rn <code>dp[n - 1]</code>, which represents the maximum amount that can be =
stolen once we reach the end of the street. Now, let=E2=80=99s think about =
what our base cases should be.</p>
<p><strong>Base cases</strong><br>
For starters, let=E2=80=99s consider what to do if there=E2=80=99s just one=
