olen is just the money in that house. So, one of our base cases is <code>dp=
[0] =3D houses[0]</code>.</p>
<p>Keep in mind that to use our DP formula (<code>dp[i] =3D max(dp[i - 1], =
houses[i] + dp[i - 2])</code>), we need to access values at indexes <code>i=
 - 1</code> and <code>i - 2</code>. This means we must set initial values f=
or index 0 and index 1. With these base cases, we can safely start using th=
e formula from index 2 onward without causing any index out-of-bound errors=
. So, what=E2=80=99s the most money that can be stolen at <code>i =3D 1</co=
de> (i.e., when there are just two adjacent houses)? We can only steal from=
 one of these houses, so <code>dp[1] =3D max(houses[0], houses[1])</code>.<=
/p>
